<td id="S1.T1.6.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">✓</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.6.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.6.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">ViCrop <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite></td>
<td id="S1.T1.6.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">✓</td>
<td id="S1.T1.6.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">✓</td>
<td id="S1.T1.6.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">✗</td></tr>
<tr id="S1.T1.6.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S1.T1.6.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S1.T1.6.1.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> (Ours)</td>
<td id="S1.T1.6.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">✓</td>
<td id="S1.T1.6.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">✓</td>
<td id="S1.T1.6.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">✓</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To tackle limitation (1), <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> leverages the internal representations of <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>, specifically their key-value (KV) caches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, to localize question-relevant image regions in a training-free manner—unlike the SEAL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> technique.
Moreover, to mitigate limitation (2), our method includes textual clues to enable object-aware <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text">localization</span> without exhaustive cropping of the image, thereby improving the algorithmic efficiency—different from DC<sup id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and ZoomEye <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.
To overcome limitation (3), <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> utilizes the cached value features readily available during inference, making it natively compatible with efficient attention implementations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>—unlike ViCrop <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> that depends on full attention weights.
Specifically, <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> combines these components as follows: for each VQA question, we first identify the target object(s) in the question prompt.
Second, we construct an object relevance map using cosine similarity between the cached text tokens of the target object(s) and the cached image tokens, and then propose relevant regions based on this map.
Third, we rank the proposed image regions based on the existence confidence of the target object in each region.
Finally, we perform <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> solely based on the image region with the highest confidence. Note that <span id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> is compatible with both global- and global-local-view <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> on the fine-grained VQA datasets V*Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, HRBench-4K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, HRBench-8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> and MME-RealWorld-Lite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.
Across the first three datasets, our method achieves on average <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42\%</annotation></semantics></math> higher accuracy over the vanilla <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> when using LLaVA-1.5 and <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17\%</annotation></semantics></math> when using LLaVA-OneVision, while improving LLaVA-OneVision by <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\%</annotation></semantics></math> on the multi-domain MME-RealWorld-Lite dataset.
Moreover, <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> achieves comparable or superior performance w.r.t. the state-of-the-art baseline ZoomEye <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> while being <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3.5\,\text{--}\,4.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.5</mn><mtext>–</mtext><mn> 4.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.5\,\text{--}\,4.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> more efficient with LLaVA-1.5 and <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\,\text{--}\,6.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mtext>–</mtext><mn> 6.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\,\text{--}\,6.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> more efficient with LLaVA-OneVision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our key contributions are as follows:
First, we propose <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span>, a training-free visual cropping method for <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>-based fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> that identifies relevant image regions using internal representations of the <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>.
Second, we provide extensive empirical evidence for <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span>’s favorable accuracy-efficiency trade-offs compared to previous visual cropping methods for fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a>.
Third, we conduct an ablation study that provides insights on how <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> leverages <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>-internal knowledge for efficient visual cropping.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> involves answering a question based on the visual content of an image.
While various types of machine learning models can be applied to this task, <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> have become the de facto standard due to their strong cross-modal reasoning capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
Here, we focus on the multiple-choice variant of <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a>, where the <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> is expected to select the correct answer from a fixed set of options.
In the following paragraphs, we describe the datasets commonly used to evaluate <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> performance on <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a></span> tasks, i.e., tasks that require focus on visual details.
Further, we provide a technical overview of recent visual cropping methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> datasets</span> 
In common <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> datasets such as Text-VQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and <span id="S2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text">Ref-COCO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, the relevant objects are typically prominent within the image.
In contrast, fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> tasks focus on much smaller visual targets.
The V*Bench dataset exemplifies this, containing significantly smaller question-relevant objects compared to the aforementioned datasets (see <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2 Related work ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Additional fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> datasets include <span id="S2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text">HRBench-4K</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, HRBench-8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, and MME-RealWorld-Lite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.
Among these, V*Bench is the only dataset that provides  <a href="#id5"><span href="#id5" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">GT</span></a> (<a href="#id5"><abbr href="#id5" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">GT</abbr></a>) annotations for question-relevant objects.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatleft" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21710v1/rel_gt_area.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/160;" width="215" height="160"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Many VQA datasets focus on large instead of tiny objects.</span> This figure shows the relative area of question-relevant objects w.r.t. the image. V*Bench contains various tiny <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a>-relevant objects.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visual cropping for fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a></span> 
In this emerging area of research, different visual cropping methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> have been proposed to improve <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> performance on fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> tasks (see <a href="#S1.T1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
SEAL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> employs a dual-<a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> setup: one MLLM for visual search and another one for the actual <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> task.
The visual search model with additional decoders is fine-tuned to predict object heatmaps and coordinates.
It performs a top-down hierarchical search, generating contextual cues to iteratively locate the target object based on confidence scores.
This approach requires task-specific fine-tuning and is rather inefficient due to its complex, multi-module design and recursive search process.
In contrast, DC<sup id="S2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> constructs a hierarchical image region tree from the global view down to regions, matching the base resolution of the vision encoder.
Each region is captioned using the <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> and those containing the target object are merged for actual <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a>.
While training-free, DC<sup id="S2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> is inefficient due to the extensive tree traversal and costly captioning process.
Similar to DC<sup id="S2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>, ZoomEye <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> employs a hierarchical tree search, but instead of captioning, it predicts a confidence score for each image region.
This score is computed through a complex mechanism involving three sequential <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> forward passes, each using a different question prompt.
As a result, the process cannot be simplified or shared across regions, making the method inefficient due to both the hierarchical search and the high cost of confidence estimation.
ViCrop <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> is an efficient, training-free visual cropping method that avoids hierarchical search by directly computing a question-guided heatmap to localize the target object.
However, its best-performing variants depend on Q-K attention weights and answer gradients, making the method incompatible with efficient attention implementations such as FlashAttention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
Unlike these methods, our method is a training-free visual cropping approach without additional modules besides the <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>.
Rather than relying on recursive search, captioning, or Q-K attention weights, we employ an informed search guided by internal representations of the <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> that is compatible with efficient attention implementations.
This design enables our method to achieve significantly higher efficiency without sacrificing accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first provide relevant background information for our method in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Background ‣ 3 FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
Next, we describe in detail how our method proposes relevant image regions based on the KV-cache in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Constructing object relevance maps from cached token similarity ‣ 3 FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.
Finally, we explain how these image regions are used for fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> tasks in <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Proposal ranking of for fine-grained ‣ 3 FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.
We provide a visualization of <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3 FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.21710v1/flow_chart.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of <span id="S3.F2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span></span>. The method identifies the target objects mentioned in the question <span id="S3.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(I)</span> and constructs their object relevance maps using cosine similarity between cached tokens <span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(II + III)</span>. Then, it proposes regions of interest and ranks those by the existence confidence of the target object in each region <span id="S3.F2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(IV + V)</span>. Finally, the selected region is used to perform <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> <span id="S3.F2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(VI)</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Background</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> typically comprise three core components: a vision encoder, a modality projector, and a  <a href="#id2"><span href="#id2" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">LLM</span></a> (<a href="#id2"><abbr href="#id2" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">LLM</abbr></a>). The <a href="#id2"><abbr href="#id2" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">LLM</abbr></a> receives an input sequence of tokens <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{X}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{X}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and predicts the next token <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> auto-regressively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
This sequence can be viewed as a concatenation of visual tokens <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{X}_{\text{vis}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{a^{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mtext>vis</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{X}_{\text{vis}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{a^{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> and instruction textual tokens <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{X}_{\text{text}}=(x_{a^{2}+1},\ldots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{X}_{\text{text}}=(x_{a^{2}+1},\ldots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> is the grid size of the vision encoder (for simplicity, we ignore system prompt tokens here).
In many models, the visual tokens from the vision encoder are extracted from a downscaled global view of the image and projected into the <a href="#id2"><abbr href="#id2" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">LLM</abbr></a>’s feature space via the modality projector.
The resizing aligns the input image with the base resolution expected by the vision encoder, e.g., <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="336\times 336" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>336</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>336</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">336\times 336</annotation></semantics></math> for CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="384\times 384" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>384</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">384\times 384</annotation></semantics></math> for SigLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.
While this regime is effective in many scenarios, it tends to fail on fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.
To address this limitation, many global-local-view <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> partition the original (unresized) image into <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> local crops in addition to the resized global view.
These crops are encoded into additional visual tokens, extending the visual input to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{X}_{\text{vis}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{a^{2}\cdot(b+1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mtext>vis</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{X}_{\text{vis}}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{a^{2}\cdot(b+1)})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This results in a rapid growth of computational cost due to the quadratic complexity of self-attention in transformer layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.
We aim to improve the performance of both global-view and global-local-view <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> on fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> tasks by constructing a map that efficiently localizes the image regions most relevant to a target object mentioned in a question. This map is referred to as the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">object relevance map</span>.
Once the relevant regions are identified, we restrict processing to these areas for the final <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> prediction, thereby improving accuracy and computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Constructing object relevance maps from cached token similarity</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Localizing objects in images remains a challenge for <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
While many models are fine-tuned on predicting bounding boxes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, they often fail when prompted directly for the location of small objects, frequently producing hallucinated or imprecise coordinates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
Instead of relying on explicit prompting, we propose to localize target objects by leveraging value features of cached tokens of <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>.
Recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> shows that visual tokens in the sequence largely preserve spatial correspondence to their originating image regions across transformer layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.
In fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, questions typically involve one or more specific objects in the image, which we refer to as the target object(s).
Since the text tokens corresponding to these targets co-exist alongside visual tokens in the token sequence, we estimate an object relevance map by computing the cosine similarity between the text and visual tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To construct this object relevance map, we first identify the text tokens corresponding to the target object(s) as shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3 FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>).
Inspired by ZoomEye <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, we use the in-context learning (ICL) capability of <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> to extract the target object(s) by providing a few examples in the prompt.
This might extract one or multiple target object(s) from the question.
For each target object, we apply a generic prompt template "<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Is there a {target object} in the image?</span>" to query the <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> alongside the image.
Due to the tokenization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> of the <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>, the target object can result in a sequence of target text tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{X}_{\text{{tgt}}}=(\hat{x}_{\text{0}},\ldots,\hat{x}_{\text{i}},\ldots,\hat{x}_{\text{s}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>X</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>tgt</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>0</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>i</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>s</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{X}_{\text{{tgt}}}=(\hat{x}_{\text{0}},\ldots,\hat{x}_{\text{i}},\ldots,\hat{x}_{\text{s}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We calculate the cosine similarity between the target tokens and the visual tokens in the sequence to construct the object relevance map.
While one might consider using standard query-key (Q-K) attention weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> for this purpose, many recent <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> employ efficient attention implementations such as FlashAttention <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, which do not generate Q-K attention weights explicitly.
We address this issue by using value features preserved in the KV-cache mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, which is commonly used to accelerate autoregressive inference by storing intermediate representations.
Leveraging this, we propose an alternative value-value (V-V) pseudo-attention approach as shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3 FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">II</span>).
For each target token <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{i}^{l},i\in\{0,\ldots,s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{i}^{l},i\in\{0,\ldots,s\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer, we compute a pseudo-attention map <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{a\times a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{a\times a}</annotation></semantics></math> by measuring its cosine similarity with the visual tokens <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{1}^{l},\ldots,x_{a}^{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{1}^{l},\ldots,x_{a}^{l})</annotation></semantics></math>, where value features are available via the KV-cache:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}=\text{reshape}(\text{cos-sim}(\hat{x}_{i}^{l},x_{j}^{l}),\left[a,a\right]),\;\;\;\text{for}\ j=1,\ldots,a^{2}\ ." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mtext>reshape</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>cos-sim</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.007em">,</mo><mtext>for</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}=\text{reshape}(\text{cos-sim}(\hat{x}_{i}^{l},x_{j}^{l}),\left[a,a\right]),\;\;\;\text{for}\ j=1,\ldots,a^{2}\ .</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, one can also compute <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> using key features, please see <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Ablation studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> for details.
In preliminary experiments, we empirically find that the pseudo-attention map <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> from a single layer might be noisy.
Therefore, we aggregate maps from <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer using attention rollout <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, incorporating residual connections to better preserve information flow as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{k=l}^{L}(\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}+\mathbf{I})/2\ ," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{k=l}^{L}(\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}+\mathbf{I})/2\ ,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐈</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the identity matrix.
We aggregate the pseudo-attention maps <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for each individual target token <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> by element-wise multiplication to capture consensus as shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3 FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> <span id="S3.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(III)</span>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{A}_{0}\odot\mathbf{A}_{1}\odot\cdots\odot\mathbf{A}_{s}\ ." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐀</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}=\mathbf{A}_{0}\odot\mathbf{A}_{1}\odot\cdots\odot\mathbf{A}_{s}\ .</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">This operation allows only regions that are consistently highlighted across all target tokens to remain prominent.
For example, the token <span id="S3.SS2.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">red</span> may highlight many red objects, but when combined with <span id="S3.SS2.p2.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">car</span>, only regions corresponding to red cars will be emphasized.
We refer to <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as the object relevance map corresponding to the target object.
A normalization of <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is performed after every matrix addition and multiplication to ensure numerical stability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For global-local-view <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>, instead of calculating cosine similarity between the target tokens and the visual tokens from the global view, we use visual tokens extracted from the local crops.
We empirically find that these local tokens can better capture fine-grained details.
We compute the pseudo-attention map <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math> using local visual tokens:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}=\text{reshape}(\text{cos-sim}(\hat{x}_{i}^{l},x_{j}^{l}),\left[h,w\right]),\;\;\;\text{for}\ j=a^{2}+1,\ldots,a^{2}\cdot(b+1)\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐀</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mtext>reshape</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>cos-sim</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.007em">,</mo><mtext>for</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}_{i}^{l}=\text{reshape}(\text{cos-sim}(\hat{x}_{i}^{l},x_{j}^{l}),\left[h,w\right]),\;\;\;\text{for}\ j=a^{2}+1,\ldots,a^{2}\cdot(b+1)\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[h,w]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[h,w]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the spatial dimensions of the local visual tokens arranged to closely preserve the original image’s aspect ratio, so that <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\cdot w=a^{2}\cdot b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\cdot w=a^{2}\cdot b</annotation></semantics></math>.
To reduce noise and enhance spatial coherence, we empirically apply a Gaussian filter to <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by downsampling, yielding a cleaner object relevance map.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Proposal ranking of <a href="#id6"><span href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</span></a> for fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an object relevance map, we define in the following a relevance score for each of its elements.
As shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3 FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (<span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IV</span>), once the object relevance map is obtained, we extract the locations of the top-<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> highest scores as anchor points on <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, which represent regions likely containing the target object.
To ensure sufficient spatial coverage, we select a relatively large
<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and enforce a minimum distance <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{dist}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>dist</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{dist}}</annotation></semantics></math> between anchor points.
Anchor points that are too close are discarded.
Then, we generate an initial symmetric <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> of minimal size <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math> per anchor point.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each initial <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> is then expanded up to the maximal size <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>, stopping when the average relevance score within the <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> falls below a predefined threshold.
We rank the resulting <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> based on the relevance score at their respective anchor points.
To eliminate redundancy, we apply non-maximum-suppression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, using a low threshold to promote diversity among selected regions.
This encourages broader spatial coverage.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The resulting object relevance map can be noisy due to spurious high-activation tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> that do not correspond to the target object.
As a result, a <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> with a high relevance score may not actually contain the target object.
Therefore, we verify whether the <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> contains the target object with the <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> as shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3 FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (<span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">V</span>).
Inspired by ZoomEye <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, we use a yes/no prompt <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"Is there a {target object} in the image?"</span> to verify whether the target object exists within each <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a>.
We compute a confidence score <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the softmax-normalized logits <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{\text{Yes}},l_{\text{No}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>Yes</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>No</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{\text{Yes}},l_{\text{No}}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the responses <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"Yes"</span> and <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"No"</span>.
Specifically, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{conf}}=2\cdot(\text{max(}\text{softmax}([l_{\text{Yes}},l_{\text{No}}]))-0.5),\;\;\;c_{\text{conf}}\in[-1,1]\;." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>max(</mtext><mtext>softmax</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>Yes</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mtext>No</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.007em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{conf}}=2\cdot(\text{max(}\text{softmax}([l_{\text{Yes}},l_{\text{No}}]))-0.5),\;\;\;c_{\text{conf}}\in[-1,1]\;.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We rerank the top <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{steps}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{steps}}</annotation></semantics></math> <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> according to their confidence of existence <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{conf}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>conf</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{conf}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the number of steps <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{steps}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{steps}}</annotation></semantics></math> controlling the  <a href="#id4"><span href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</span></a> (<a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a>) budget.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Final <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> inference</span> 
In the previous paragraphs, we demonstrated how to obtain the most relevant <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> for each target object.
These <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> are now passed to the <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> for the final VQA prediction.
We follow the inference strategy from ZoomEye <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, which categorizes questions in fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> datasets into two types.
Type-1 questions involve single-object instances, e.g., "<span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">What is the color of the car?</span>" or "<span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">What is the relative position of the ball to the bench?</span>".
Type-2 questions concern multiple instances of an object type, e.g., "<span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">How many bikes are in the image?</span>".
These categories can be automatically inferred using ICL or keyword-based heuristics, without requiring prior knowledge of the question.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For type-1 questions, we select the <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> with the highest confidence score for each target object.
If the question involves multiple target objects, we combine the regions covering all relevant objects for the final <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a>.
For type-2 questions, we iterate over all proposed <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> and select those with confidence scores higher than a threshold.
This step is not constrained by the <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{steps}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{steps}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of global-local-view <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>, this process slightly differs.
Instead of combining the visual crops into a single area, we utilize the model’s text-image-interleaved capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
Specifically, we provide one global image view—where all target objects are visually highlighted—alongside the top-confidence <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> for each target object.
Please check <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Additional implementation details of FOCUS ‣ Appendix A Implementation details ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> for details.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first describe the implementation details of our experiments in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Implementation details ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and provide the results on fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> datasets in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Results on fine-grained datasets ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
Further, we verify our design choices in an ablation study in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Ablation studies ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.
Finally, we show some qualitative examples and discuss the limitations of <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> in <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Qualitative examples ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> and <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Limitations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21710v1/pareto.svg" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/193;" width="476" height="193"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">FOCUS<span id="S4.F3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif"> is at the Pareto front on fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> benchmarks.</span></span> Given the same computation budget, <span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> (purple crosses) significantly outperforms other visual cropping methods, on three different datasets and for two model architectures. It achieves <math id="S4.F3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3\,\text{--}\,6.5\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mtext>–</mtext><mn> 6.5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\,\text{--}\,6.5\times</annotation></semantics></math> higher efficiency than the best-performing baseline ZoomEye. Note that we show only a limited set of data points for each method to ensure a clear visualization. The full results are available in <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Full results ‣ Appendix C Additional results ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Implementation details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, we evaluate <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> on several fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> benchmarks: V*Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, HRBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, and MME-RealWorld-Lite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.
We report accuracy on multiple-choice questions as the primary performance metric.
Another critical consideration is the trade-off between performance and efficiency, as inference with <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> can be computationally expensive.
In the case of multiple-choice <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a>, inference speed is largely determined by the sequence length during the prefill phase <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.
Since the sequence length remains relatively consistent across different searches, we quantify efficiency using the number of  <a href="#id4"><span href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</span></a> (<a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a>) required for the visual search.
We provide details of used hardware and on the calculation all metrics in <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Hardware specifications ‣ Appendix A Implementation details ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> and <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Metrics ‣ Appendix A Implementation details ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>.
We evaluate our method using two types of <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>: LLaVA-1.5-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, a global-view <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>, and LLaVA-OneVision-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, a global-local-view <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>.
As described in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Constructing object relevance maps from cached token similarity ‣ 3 FOCUS: Fine-grained visual object cropping using cached token similarity ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we utilize representations from multiple layers to compute the object relevance map.
For LLaVA-1.5, we use representations from the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>14</mn><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32^{\text{nd}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>32</mn><mtext>nd</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32^{\text{nd}}</annotation></semantics></math> layer (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=14</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=32</annotation></semantics></math>), and for LLaVA-OneVision from the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>14</mn><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>28</mn><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> layer (<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=14</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=28</annotation></semantics></math>).
To evaluate performance under varying computational budgets, we adjust only the number of steps, setting <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{steps}}\in\{1,2,3,4,6,8\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{steps}}\in\{1,2,3,4,6,8\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We describe the configuration of the remaining hyperparameters of <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> in <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Hyperparameters of FOCUS ‣ Appendix A Implementation details ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For ZoomEye, we vary the number of crops per layer and the cropping depth.
For ViCrop, we report results from its best-performing variants, i.e., <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rel-attn</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">attn-grad</span>.
For DC<sup id="S4.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>, we determine the <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a> via the base resolution of the vision encoder.
For SEAL, we evaluate the publicly available pre-trained model without modifications.
Additional implementation details are provided in <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Hyperparameters of recent visual cropping methods ‣ Appendix A Implementation details ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.
Note that SEAL and ZoomEye use a different inference scheme on V*Bench compared to the open-ended generation approach of <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span>.
With this alternative scheme, we observe significantly improved performance with ZoomEye on V*Bench.
Further, we also observe a notable gap between our ZoomEye results and those reported in the original paper.
Detailed results are provided in <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Inference scheme and performance discrepancy ‣ Appendix C Additional results ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results on fine-grained <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> datasets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments with LLaVA-1.5 on V*Bench, HRBench-4K, and HRBench-8K (see <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><a href="#S4.F3" title="In 4 Experiments ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></span>).
Overall, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> outperforms the four other visual cropping methods on a relatively small computational budget of fewer than <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>17</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17</annotation></semantics></math> <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a>.
<span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> achieves an accuracy of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="72.77\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>72.77</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">72.77\%</annotation></semantics></math> on V*Bench, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="51.75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>51.75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">51.75\%</annotation></semantics></math> on HRBench-4K, and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45.00\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45.00</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45.00\%</annotation></semantics></math> on HRBench-8K.
SEAL achieves a slightly higher accuracy than <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> on V*Bench, but at the cost of significantly lower efficiency due to its multi-module design and recursive visual search.
On high-resolution datasets like HRBench, its performance is only on par with the vanilla <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>.
We suspect this decline stems from SEAL’s training data, which is specifically optimized for resolutions below 2K.
ZoomEye achieves the highest accuracy on V*Bench (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="77.48\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>77.48</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">77.48\%</annotation></semantics></math>), but only with an extremely deep tree search, resulting in substantial computational overhead.
At our top accuracy of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="72.77\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>72.77</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">72.77\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> is <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="3.43\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.43</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.43\times</annotation></semantics></math> more efficient than ZoomEye.
On HRBench-4K, our method not only surpasses ZoomEye with a better top accuracy but also uses <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4.39\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.39</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.39\times</annotation></semantics></math> fewer <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a>.
On HRBench-8K, while ZoomEye attains a higher top accuracy of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="49.00\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>49.00</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">49.00\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> achieves <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="44.88\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>44.88</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44.88\%</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="4.72\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.72</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.72\times</annotation></semantics></math> greater efficiency.
This performance gap on HRBench-8K is likely due to the limitations of the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24\times 24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>24</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24\times 24</annotation></semantics></math> object relevance map produced by LLaVA-1.5.
Smaller objects often remain undetected, limiting our model’s ability to improve accuracy—even when increasing <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{steps}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{steps}}</annotation></semantics></math> to allocate more computation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S4.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results on MME-RealWorld-Lite.</span> We provide the accuracy for each task. Further, we report the average accuracy and <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a> per subset for efficiency comparison. The dataset includes the domains <span id="S4.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">OCR (Optical Character Recognition)</span>, <span id="S4.T2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">RS (Remote Sensing)</span>, <span id="S4.T2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DT (Diagram and Table)</span>, <span id="S4.T2.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MO (Monitoring)</span>, and <span id="S4.T2.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AD (Autonomous Driving)</span>.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T2.14" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:41.3pt;vertical-align:-19.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-133.4pt,16.0pt) scale(0.563872264449197,0.563872264449197) ;">
<table id="S4.T2.14.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.T2.14.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.14.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"></td>
<td id="S4.T2.14.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;" colspan="7"><span id="S4.T2.14.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Perception</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.14.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"></td>
<td id="S4.T2.14.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;" colspan="6"><span id="S4.T2.14.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Reasoning</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.14.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T2.14.1.2.1" class="ltx_td" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"></td>
<td id="S4.T2.14.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;" colspan="5"><span id="S4.T2.14.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Sub-task accuracy</span><span id="S4.T2.14.1.2.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> </span><math id="S4.T2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[\%]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">[</mo><mo>%</mo><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\%]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
