<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Metrics</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we provide a detailed description of our performance and efficiency metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance metrics</span> 
The main performance metric is the accuracy of visual cropping methods, which is typically reported as a percentage.
We follow the standard evaluation protocol and do not apply any post-processing to the answers generated by the visual cropping methods using the <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>.
This means that if the ground-truth label is <span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"A"</span> and the model outputs <span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"The answer is A"</span> or <span id="A1.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"(A)"</span>, we do not extract or normalize the answer to match the label.
As a result, such responses are counted as incorrect. However, this type of mismatch is of minor importance in our experiments: across all models and three datasets (V*Bench, HRBench-4K, HRBench-8K), we did not observe any irregular or non-standard response formats.
We compute the average accuracy as the unweighted mean over all <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> samples in a dataset, i.e., as <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=1}^{N}(\hat{y}_{i}=y_{i})/N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=1}^{N}(\hat{y}_{i}=y_{i})/N</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted answer and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the ground-truth label for the <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th sample.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency metrics</span> 
An important metric is the number of  <a href="#id4"><span href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</span></a> (<a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a>) required for the visual search. Note that we exclude the <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a> needed for the final <a href="#id3"><abbr href="#id3" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">VQA</abbr></a> prediction, as different inference schemes can generate different forward passes, see <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Inference scheme and performance discrepancy ‣ Appendix C Additional results ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>. We compute this by tracking how often the <span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">generate</span>, <span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">forward</span>, or <span id="A1.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">__call__</span> methods of the respective <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> are invoked per question. For methods that use multiple <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a> (e.g., SEAL), we report the total number of <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a> of all <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Another key metric we report is the efficiency improvement of <span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> relative to the baseline methods.
Among all evaluated approaches, <span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> demonstrates the highest efficiency.
To ensure a fair comparison, we consider the top accuracy achieved by <span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> and the best-performing baseline, and select the lower of the two as the reference accuracy.
For both methods, we determine the number of  <a href="#id4"><span href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</span></a> (<a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a>) required to reach this reference accuracy—either by taking the exact value or interpolating between data points to estimate the <a href="#id4"><abbr href="#id4" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">FP</abbr></a>.
This yields <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FP}_{\text{ours}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>FP</mtext><mtext>ours</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FP}_{\text{ours}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <span id="A1.SS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> and <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FP}_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>FP</mtext><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FP}_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the best-performing baseline.
The relative efficiency improvement is then computed as <math id="A1.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FP}_{\text{ref}}/\text{FP}_{\text{ours}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>FP</mtext><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mtext>FP</mtext><mtext>ours</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FP}_{\text{ref}}/\text{FP}_{\text{ours}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, we report two additional metrics to provide a practical comparison of efficiency, i.e., execution time and peak GPU memory usage (see <a href="#A3.T10" title="In C.2 Additional efficiency metrics ‣ Appendix C Additional results ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).
Execution time is recorded per sample, and we report the average time per question across a dataset.
Peak memory is measured using <span id="A1.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()</span> and converted to GB by dividing the result by <math id="A1.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1024^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>1024</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1024^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Hyperparameters of <span id="A1.SS3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span></h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection outlines the hyperparameters used in our method, <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span>. These parameters are applied consistently across all experiments and correspond to the results reported in <a href="#S4" title="4 Experiments ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
As noted in the main paper, the only parameter we vary is the number of steps, <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{steps}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{steps}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Most other hyperparameters remain fixed across experiments for both LLaVA-1.5 and LLaVA-OneVision.
The exceptions are three parameters: <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{dist}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>dist</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{dist}}</annotation></semantics></math> (specific to LLaVA-1.5), and <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> (specific to LLaVA-OneVision), as detailed in <a href="#A1.T5" title="In A.3 Hyperparameters of FOCUS ‣ Appendix A Implementation details ‣ FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For LLaVA-1.5, we use a smaller value of <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{steps}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>steps</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{steps}}</annotation></semantics></math> is low to reduce the number of proposed <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a>.
Additionally, we increase <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{dist}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>dist</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{dist}}</annotation></semantics></math> on HRBench to ensure a broader spatial distribution of the <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a> across the image.
In contrast, for LLaVA-OneVision, we set a larger <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> due to its higher-resolution object relevance map.
Moreover, we set <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{max}}=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{max}}=9</annotation></semantics></math> for V*Bench and <math id="A1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{max}}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{max}}=5</annotation></semantics></math> for the other datasets, as V*Bench contains lower-resolution (2K) images.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The hyperparameters used in <span id="A1.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span> are generally robust and transferable across a wide range of use cases. For users applying our method to new datasets, we recommend adjusting <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which determines the maximum size of each proposed <a href="#id6"><abbr href="#id6" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ROI</abbr></a>, based on both the resolution of the input images and the spatial resolution of the object relevance map. In particular, <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> should be chosen so that the corresponding region in the original image spans approximately <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times</annotation></semantics></math> the base resolution of the vision encoder. For example, if the object relevance map has a spatial resolution of <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60\times 30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60\times 30</annotation></semantics></math>, and the input image resolution is <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7680\times 3840" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7680</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3840</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7680\times 3840</annotation></semantics></math>, then each grid element corresponds to an area of <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128\times 128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>128</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128\times 128</annotation></semantics></math>. Setting <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{max}}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{max}}=5</annotation></semantics></math> yields a maximum crop size of <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="640\times 640" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>640</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>640</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">640\times 640</annotation></semantics></math>, which falls within the recommended range of <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="384\times 384-768\times 768" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>384</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>384</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>768</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>768</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">384\times 384-768\times 768</annotation></semantics></math> for the SigLIP encoder. Additionally, if one considers migrating our method to another <a href="#id1"><abbr href="#id1" title="FOCUS: Internal MLLM Representations forEfficient Fine-Grained Visual Question Answering" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLLM</abbr></a>, we recommend selecting the last <math id="A1.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\%-60\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>25</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\%-60\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the layers.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span><span id="A1.T5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameters of <span id="A1.T5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FOCUS</span>.</span></figcaption>
